# BadaBhai vernacular alias overlay — the words workers actually use for their trade.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. The published catalogue answers "what does the Government of
# India call this occupation". It does not answer "what does a worker call it". All 8,695
# scraped aliases are lang='en' official titles ("Welder, Gas"; "Cook, Domestic"), and 77%
# of selectable occupations carry exactly one. A worker who says "kharad ka kaam karta
# hun" matches nothing in that catalogue. Every line below is a bridge from a worker's
# own words to a published code.
#
# FORMAT. One alias per line, `"kind":"alias"`. `source` defaults to 'rvm' and may not be
# anything else — an authored alias must never be able to claim it came from a standard.
#   {"kind":"alias","job_domain_id":"jd_nco_7212_0301","text":"वेल्डर","lang":"hi"}
#
# REVIEW. `git blame` on a line names the reviewer who signed it. That is the entire
# provenance mechanism and it is why this file is line-per-alias rather than a JSON array
# — a reindented array makes blame meaningless.
#
# THE ONE RULE THAT IS NOT OBVIOUS: every alias must survive normalization DISTINCTLY.
# `normalizeOccupationText` strips Indian occupational particles, so "welding wala",
# "welding ka kaam" and "welding" all collapse to `welding`. Only one of them can win
# `is_searchable`; the rest are unreachable rows that still cost an embedding. Author the
# STEM, never the particle-laden phrase. `validateAliasOverlay` enforces this, so a
# padded corpus fails the seed rather than quietly inflating the alias count.
#
# COVERAGE. This first tranche covers the highest worker-volume trades, in the order the
# plan prioritises them: welding, tailoring, driving, cooking, electrical, masonry,
# machining, security, housekeeping, construction helpers, plumbing, painting, carpentry.
# It is deliberately NOT the full ~3,000: see the Phase 2 PR for what the remaining
# breadth needs (a provider key for the LLM batch, and real chat_messages for the miner).
#
# PRIVACY. Occupation words only. No worker-derived text may enter this file without
# going through the pseudonymizer first — the miner is the path that could carry PII, and
# the validator rejects digits, '@' and URLs as a structural backstop.

# ── BARE COMMON WORDS — these exist to WIN AT L0 BEFORE L1 IS TRIED ─────────
# Every entry here was added because the eval harness caught the L1 skeleton folding a
# common worker word onto an unrelated occupation. The skeleton is lossy by design
# (kh->k, w->v, vowels dropped), and on short words it over-folds:
#
#   driver  -> "drvr" == Drover      (jd_isco_6121, cattle herder)
#   fitter  -> "ftr"  == Father      (jd_nco_2636_0100, religious professional)
#   painter -> "pntr" == Pantry Man  (jd_nco_5120_0600)
#
# The resolver exhausts L0 at every span length before trying L1 at any length, so an
# EXACT alias for the bare word makes the collision unreachable rather than merely
# unlikely. That ordering is the fix; these rows are how it gets used. Removing one of
# these lines silently reintroduces the collision, so do not "tidy" them away as
# redundant with the longer forms.
{"kind":"alias","job_domain_id":"jd_nco_8322_0100","text":"driver","lang":"en"}
{"kind":"alias","job_domain_id":"jd_nco_7233_0101","text":"fitter","lang":"en"}
{"kind":"alias","job_domain_id":"jd_nco_7131_0100","text":"painter","lang":"en"}
