# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# BATCH 1 ROLLOUT — CAD draughting and CAM part programming.
#
# STATUS: DRAFTED, NOT YET RATIFIED. Authored alongside the qp_cad_drafting and
# qp_cam_programming packs and routed for human sign-off with them. The git blame on these
# lines names the drafter; it does not yet name a ratifier.
#
# WHY IT EXISTS. Neither pack was reachable at all. Measured before this block landed:
#   cad, cad operator                 -> Caddie (golf, jd_nco_9621_0300), at L1
#   cad designer, mechanical designer -> Designer (Garment, jd_nco_7532_0100), at L0
#   post processor                    -> Mail Carriers (jd_isco_4412), at L1
#   autocad solidworks catia creo draughtsman mastercam powermill solidcam edgecam
#   part programming cam programmer   -> NO MATCH
#   कैड ऑटोकैड ड्राफ्ट्समैन नक्शा कैम प्रोग्रामर सीएनसी प्रोग्रामर -> NO MATCH; the Devanagari half was 100% absent
#
# Every row below is an L0 EXACT key, and that is the entire mechanism. L0 is exhausted at
# every span length before L1 is tried at any length, so an exact row makes a skeleton
# collision unreachable rather than merely unlikely — the same fix, for the same reason, as
# the driver/fitter/painter rows at the top of this file.
#
# WHAT A BARE ROW COSTS, AND THE RULE THIS BLOCK NOW FOLLOWS. An L0 row fixes the exact word
# and simultaneously claims that word's SKELETON for its domain, which moves every fuzzy
# neighbour of the word onto the same domain. So a bare row is authored only when (a) the
# skeleton has no other trade claimant, or (b) the neighbours it captures are guarded by
# their own L0 rows. Bare "fusion" failed both and was pulled; bare "cad" passes only with
# the "caddy" and "g code" guards below; bare "draughtsman" and "नक्शा" pass but belong to
# the unit ROUTER rather than to the mechanical role, because they are what a civil and an
# electrical draughtsman say too. Every one of these is a measurement, not a judgement —
# packages/db/src/question-pack-reachability.test.ts re-runs them through the real index.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ── CAD draughting — NCO 3118.0401 (Draughtsperson, Mechanical) ─────────────
# "cad" is the driver/fitter/painter case, with one honest difference from those three.
# skeletonKey drops interior vowels and collapses repeats, so cad -> "cd" and
# caddie -> "cdd" -> "cd" are ONE key, and the golf caddie owned it. The bare exact row
# takes the exact word "cad" back at L0 — but it does NOT pre-empt the fold, it REVERSES
# its owner: the "cd" bucket now holds 3118.0401 first and 9621.0300 second, so every
# NON-exact spelling that folds to "cd" moves from the caddie to the drawing office. That
# is a trade, not a fix, and the two rows it actually costs are guarded below and in the
# CAM block ("caddy", "g code"). What is left in the bucket is bare "cod"/"cud"/"cadi" —
# words no worker types as a trade — and it is written down here rather than claimed away.
# Do NOT tidy the bare row away as redundant with "cad operator": deleting it puts every
# bare "cad" back on a golf course, silently.
{"kind":"alias","job_domain_id":"jd_nco_3118_0401","text":"cad","lang":"en"}
{"kind":"alias","job_domain_id":"jd_nco_3118_0401","text":"cad operator","lang":"en"}
